(window.__PQ=window.__PQ||{})["01-linux"]={"slug":"01-linux","title":"Linux Fundamentals","accent":"#fbbf24","sections":[{"id":"s01-filesystem-navigation","title":"Filesystem and navigation","c":[{"id":"s01-filesystem-navigation-c1","t":"The directory tree","p":"There is one tree, not drive letters. Everything hangs below /. Standard places keep programs, settings, logs and your own files apart so that tools and people know where to look.","k":[["Where would you look for a service's configuration?","Under `/etc`, usually in a file or folder named after the service."],["Where do container runtimes keep image layers?","Typically under `/var/lib`, for example `/var/lib/docker` or `/var/lib/containerd`."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-filesystem-navigation-c2","t":"Finding files","p":"find walks the tree and tests each file against conditions you give it. grep searches inside files. locate searches a prebuilt index and is instant but can be out of date.","k":[],"o":[["find","you need conditions such as age, size, owner or permissions","you only know part of the file's content"],["locate","you want a fast name lookup and the index is fresh","the file was created in the last day, or you need current state"],["grep","you are searching inside file contents","you are looking for file names or metadata"]]},{"id":"s01-filesystem-navigation-c3","t":"Links and inodes","p":"A file has two parts: its data and a record called an inode that stores size, owner, permissions and where the data lives. The name in a folder is just a pointer to an inode. A hard link is another name for the same inode. A symbolic link is a small file that holds a path.","k":[["Why is \"current -> releases/2026-10-30\" a symlink and not a hard link?","Directories cannot be hard linked, and the symlink target can be switched atomically to deploy or roll back."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s01-filesystem-navigation-c4","t":"Archives and compression","p":"tar bundles many files into one file (an archive). A compressor such as gzip, xz or zstd makes it smaller. They are separate steps that tar can chain for you.","k":[],"o":[["gzip","you want the most compatible format and quick speed","you need the smallest size"],["xz","size matters more than time, such as releases","you compress often or need speed"],["zstd","you want fast and well compressed, modern default","the receiving system is very old"]]}]},{"id":"s02-users-groups-permissions","title":"Users, groups and permissions","c":[{"id":"s02-users-groups-permissions-c1","t":"Users and groups","p":"Every process runs as a user, and every user belongs to one or more groups. The system knows users by number: a UID for the user and a GID for the group. Names are only labels for those numbers.","k":[["What does the kernel actually store on a file, the owner's name or number?","The number (UID and GID). Names are looked up from /etc/passwd and /etc/group when you list files."],["Why give a web server its own system user?","A compromised server can then only reach what that user may reach, not the whole system."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-users-groups-permissions-c2","t":"Permission bits","p":"Each file has three sets of three switches: read, write and execute, for the owner, the group and everyone else. ls -l shows them as -rwxr-x---.","k":[["A directory is `rw-r--r--`. Why can you not `cd` into it?","It has no execute bit, and on a directory x is what allows entering it."],["What is 640 in letters?","rw-r----- (owner read and write, group read, others none)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-users-groups-permissions-c3","t":"Special bits and umask","p":"Three extra bits change how files and directories behave, and umask decides the permissions new files start with.","k":[["Why does a shared project directory usually get setgid?","So files created inside belong to the project group no matter who creates them, and teammates keep access."],["What file mode does umask 077 give new files?","600, since 666 minus 077 is 600."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s02-users-groups-permissions-c4","t":"sudo and least privilege","p":"sudo lets a permitted user run one command with another user's rights, usually root, while logging who did it. Least privilege means giving each person or program only the access the job needs.","k":[["Why use visudo instead of a normal editor?","It validates the syntax before saving; a broken sudoers file can lock all admins out."]],"o":[["Specific command in sudoers","automation or a person needs one or two actions","they need to explore the system freely"],["Membership of an admin group","a trusted admin needs broad rights","this is a service account or a shared login"],["Separate account per person","you need an audit trail","you plan to share one login for convenience"]]},{"id":"s02-users-groups-permissions-c5","t":"Access control lists (ACLs)","p":"An ACL adds extra permission entries to a file for specific people or groups, beyond the owner, group and everyone-else slots.","k":[],"o":[["Plain groups","the audience is stable and used in many places","one-off access to a single path"],["ACLs","you need an exception for a specific person or folder","you can solve it with a group; ACLs are harder to audit"]]}]},{"id":"s03-processes-systemd","title":"Processes, signals and systemd","c":[{"id":"s03-processes-systemd-c1","t":"What a process is","p":"A process is a program that is running. The kernel gives it an ID (PID), its own memory, a list of open files, and a user identity. New processes are created by an existing process asking the kernel to fork a copy and then exec a different program into it.","k":[["Why does it matter that PID 1 handles signals correctly in a container?","The runtime sends the stop signal to PID 1; if it ignores it, the container waits and is then killed after the grace period."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-processes-systemd-c2","t":"Process states and zombies","p":"At any moment a process is running, waiting, sleeping, stopped or finished-but-not-collected. The one letter in the STAT column of ps tells you which.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-processes-systemd-c3","t":"Signals","p":"A signal is a short message sent to a process: stop, reload, or you have done something illegal. Most can be caught so the program can tidy up. Two cannot.","k":[["Which signal can a program never handle?","SIGKILL (9) and SIGSTOP; the kernel acts on them directly."],["Why does a container that ignores SIGTERM take 30 seconds to stop?","The orchestrator waits the grace period and then sends SIGKILL."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-processes-systemd-c4","t":"Foreground, background and job control","p":"Your shell can run one program in front of you and many behind it. & starts a command in the background, Ctrl-Z pauses the foreground one, and bg/fg move jobs back and forth.","k":[],"o":[["Foreground","a quick task you want to watch","it takes longer than your connection is reliable"],["tmux / screen","an interactive, long-lived session","the job must start at boot or restart on failure"],["systemd service","anything that should run unattended","a one-off command you will never repeat"]]},{"id":"s03-processes-systemd-c5","t":"systemd services","p":"systemd is the first process (PID 1) on most Linux servers. It starts services in the right order, restarts them when they fail, and collects their logs. A unit file describes one service.","k":[["What does Restart=on-failure not restart?","A service that exits cleanly with status 0 or was stopped by an administrator."],["Why set User= in a unit file?","So the service does not run as root; a compromise then stays inside that user's permissions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s03-processes-systemd-c6","t":"systemd timers","p":"A timer unit starts a service on a schedule, like cron but with logs, dependencies and the ability to catch up after the machine was off.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s04-packages-software","title":"Packages and software","c":[{"id":"s04-packages-software-c1","t":"Package managers","p":"A package is a program plus its metadata: version, files, and the other packages it needs. A package manager installs it, works out dependencies, and can remove or upgrade it cleanly.","k":[["Does apt update install anything?","No. It only refreshes the list of available packages; apt upgrade installs newer versions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-packages-software-c2","t":"Repositories and signing keys","p":"A repository is a signed catalogue of packages on a server. Your machine trusts it only if you have installed that repository's public signing key.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s04-packages-software-c3","t":"Versions, pinning and updates","p":"Servers should run known versions and receive security fixes on purpose, not by surprise. Pinning fixes a package at a version; holding stops it from upgrading.","k":[],"o":[["Automatic security updates","a stateless fleet with fast rollback","a single stateful server you cannot restart safely"],["Scheduled patch windows","regulated or stateful systems","you cannot tolerate a growing vulnerability backlog"],["Immutable images rebuilt regularly","containers and autoscaling groups","the host cannot be replaced easily"]]},{"id":"s04-packages-software-c4","t":"Building from source","p":"When no package exists, or you need a special build option, you can compile the program yourself. The classic recipe is configure, make, install.","k":[],"o":[["Distribution package","it exists and the version is acceptable","you need a newer feature than it offers"],["Vendor repository","the vendor signs and maintains packages","the vendor's repository is unmaintained"],["Container image","you want identical builds everywhere","the tool must be part of the host operating system"],["Source build","nothing else fits and you can maintain it","you cannot track security fixes yourself"]]}]},{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools","title":"Shell, pipes and text tools","c":[{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools-c1","t":"Standard streams and redirection","p":"Every program starts with three channels: standard input (0) where it reads, standard output (1) where it writes results, and standard error (2) where it writes problems. The shell can point each one at a file or at another program.","k":[["What does `cmd > file 2>&1` do compared to `cmd 2>&1 > file`?","The first sends both streams to the file. The second points errors at the terminal first and only then redirects output, so errors stay on the screen."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools-c2","t":"Pipes and exit codes","p":"A pipeline runs all its programs at once, each feeding the next. Every command also finishes with an exit code: 0 means success and any other number means a failure.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools-c3","t":"Searching with grep","p":"grep prints the lines that match a pattern. It is the quickest way to find the needle in a log haystack.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools-c4","t":"Transforming with sed, cut, sort and uniq","p":"sed edits a stream of text line by line. cut takes columns. sort orders lines. uniq -c counts neighbours that are identical, which is why it is almost always used after sort.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools-c5","t":"awk for columns and calculations","p":"awk reads lines, splits them into fields ($1, $2, ...), and runs small programs on them. It is a tiny spreadsheet for text.","k":[],"o":[["grep","you want whole lines that match","you need to compute or rearrange columns"],["sed","you want a substitution or a range of lines","you need arithmetic or grouping"],["awk","you need columns, conditions, sums or counts","the job needs real data structures; use a script language"],["cut","one simple fixed column or character range","the delimiter is irregular, such as repeated spaces"]]},{"id":"s05-shell-text-tools-c6","t":"JSON with jq, and parallel work with xargs","p":"Modern tools return JSON, which the line-oriented tools handle badly. jq is \"awk for JSON\". xargs turns a list of lines into arguments for another command, and can run many at once.","k":[["Why use find -print0 with xargs -0?","File names can contain spaces or newlines; NUL separators are the only safe delimiter."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s06-bash-scripting","title":"Bash scripting","c":[{"id":"s06-bash-scripting-c1","t":"Variables, quoting and expansion","p":"A variable stores text. The shell replaces $name with its value before running the command, and it splits unquoted values on spaces. Quoting controls that splitting.","k":[["Why quote \"$var\" almost everywhere?","Unquoted values are split on whitespace and expanded as globs, which breaks on spaces and can match unintended files."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-bash-scripting-c2","t":"Tests and conditionals","p":"if runs a command and branches on its exit code. [[ ... ]] is the bash test for strings, numbers and files.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-bash-scripting-c3","t":"Loops and reading input","p":"Loops repeat work: over a list of items, over lines of a file, or until a condition is met.","k":[["Why use read -r?","Without -r, backslashes are interpreted and mangle the input."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-bash-scripting-c4","t":"Functions and structure","p":"A function groups commands under a name. Well-structured scripts define small functions, then a main that calls them.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s06-bash-scripting-c5","t":"Safe scripts: strict mode, traps and temp files","p":"Strict mode makes a script stop at the first problem. A trap runs cleanup code however the script ends. mktemp creates unpredictable temporary files.","k":[],"o":[["Bash","gluing commands, small file and process tasks","parsing JSON deeply, complex logic, tests"],["Python","structured data, APIs, retries, unit tests","a three-line pipeline"]]},{"id":"s06-bash-scripting-c6","t":"Arguments, options and exit codes","p":"Real scripts take options such as -f file or --dry-run. getopts parses short options, and a consistent exit code tells callers what happened.","k":[["Which exit code means the caller used the script wrongly?","2 by convention; 1 is a general runtime failure."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s07-networking-tools","title":"Linux networking tools","c":[{"id":"s07-networking-tools-c1","t":"Interfaces, addresses and routes","p":"A machine talks through interfaces (such as eth0), each with one or more IP addresses. The routing table decides which interface and next hop a packet uses.","k":[["What does \"default via 10.0.0.1 dev eth0\" mean?","Traffic with no more specific route is sent to the gateway 10.0.0.1 through eth0."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-networking-tools-c2","t":"Sockets and listening ports","p":"A socket is one end of a conversation: an IP address and port. A service listens on a port, and every client connection is another socket.","k":[["A service works with curl localhost but not from another host. First thing to check?","ss -tlnp: it may be bound to 127.0.0.1 only, or a firewall may block the port."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-networking-tools-c3","t":"DNS troubleshooting","p":"DNS turns names into addresses. Many outages that look like network failures are DNS failures.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-networking-tools-c4","t":"Testing connections layer by layer","p":"Test from the bottom up: link, address, route, DNS, TCP port, TLS, HTTP. The first layer that fails is the layer to fix.","k":[],"o":[["ping","you want a quick reachability hint","ICMP is filtered; use nc"],["nc -vz","you need to know if a TCP port opens","you need the HTTP status"],["curl -v","you need the full HTTP exchange","you only need the port state"],["mtr","loss or latency happens somewhere along the path","the target is on the same subnet"]]},{"id":"s07-networking-tools-c5","t":"Packet capture with tcpdump","p":"tcpdump records the packets crossing an interface. It is the final authority on what really went over the wire.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s07-networking-tools-c6","t":"Firewalls: nftables and iptables","p":"A host firewall decides which packets are accepted, dropped or rewritten. nftables is the modern tool; iptables is the older interface that many systems and container runtimes still use.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s08-storage","title":"Storage, filesystems and LVM","c":[{"id":"s08-storage-c1","t":"Block devices, partitions and filesystems","p":"A block device is raw storage (/dev/nvme0n1). A partition is a slice of it. A filesystem is the structure (files, folders, permissions) written onto it so the kernel can present it as a tree.","k":[["Why mount by UUID?","Device names like /dev/sdb depend on detection order and can change; the UUID is stored in the filesystem itself."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-storage-c2","t":"Filesystems: ext4 and XFS","p":"Both are journalling filesystems: they write intended changes to a log first, so a crash does not corrupt the structure.","k":[],"o":[["ext4","you may need to shrink volumes or want maximum compatibility","you expect billions of small files"],["XFS","large volumes, databases, RHEL family defaults","you expect to reduce the size later"]]},{"id":"s08-storage-c3","t":"Logical Volume Manager (LVM)","p":"LVM adds a flexible layer between disks and filesystems. You pool disks into a volume group, then carve out logical volumes that can grow, shrink or move without repartitioning.","k":[["You added a disk to the VG. What is left to do to give the database more room?","Extend the logical volume and its filesystem, for example lvextend -r."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-storage-c4","t":"Full disks and inodes","p":"A filesystem can be \"full\" in two ways: out of space (bytes) or out of inodes (file records). Deleted files can also still hold space if a process keeps them open.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s08-storage-c5","t":"RAID, mounts and quotas","p":"RAID combines disks for speed or redundancy. Quotas cap how much a user or project may store. Mount options tune behaviour.","k":[["Is RAID a backup?","No. It protects against disk failure only; accidental deletion, corruption and ransomware are replicated to every disk."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting","title":"Performance and troubleshooting","c":[{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting-c1","t":"The USE method","p":"For every resource, check Utilisation (how busy), Saturation (how much work is waiting) and Errors. The resource with high saturation or errors is your bottleneck.","k":[["What does a high load average with low CPU use usually mean?","Tasks are waiting, often on disk or network storage (uninterruptible sleep), not on the CPU."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting-c2","t":"CPU and load","p":"Load average is the number of tasks running or waiting, averaged over 1, 5 and 15 minutes. It must be compared with the number of CPU cores.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting-c3","t":"Memory, swap and the OOM killer","p":"Linux uses spare memory as a file cache, so \"free\" looks small on a healthy system. The number that matters is available memory. When it runs out, the kernel kills a process.","k":[["In a Kubernetes pod the app is OOMKilled but the node has free memory. Why?","The container exceeded its own memory limit; the cgroup enforces it regardless of node memory."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting-c4","t":"Disk I/O","p":"Slow storage often looks like a slow application. iostat shows how busy a device is and how long requests wait.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting-c5","t":"Tracing with strace, lsof and the journal","p":"When metrics show where the pressure is, tracing shows what the process is doing. strace lists every system call, lsof lists open files and sockets, and the journal holds what the program said.","k":[["A process shows 0% CPU but never answers. Which tool shows what it is waiting for?","strace -p PID (blocked system call) or cat /proc/PID/wchan; lsof shows the files and sockets involved."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s09-performance-troubleshooting-c6","t":"A troubleshooting playbook","p":"Combine the method and tools into a fixed order: define the symptom, check recent changes, find the constrained resource, find the responsible process, fix, and write it down.","k":[],"o":[["uptime and vmstat","you need a broad first look","you already know it is the network"],["iostat","load is high and CPU is low","the problem is clearly inside the app"],["ss and tcpdump","connections fail or time out","the host is out of memory"],["strace","one process behaves oddly","the entire host is slow"]]}]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening","title":"Security hardening","c":[{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c1","t":"Defence in depth for a single host","p":"No single control stops every attack, so you stack several. If one layer fails, the next one limits the damage.","k":[["Why keep logs on another host?","An attacker with root can edit local logs, so a remote copy keeps the evidence."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c2","t":"Hardening SSH","p":"SSH is the door into almost every Linux server. The defaults are reasonable, but a few settings turn it from \"guessable\" into \"key holders only\".","k":[["Why use sshd -T instead of reading the file?","It prints the effective configuration after all Include files and defaults, which is what the daemon really uses."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c3","t":"SSH keys, agents and certificates","p":"An SSH key pair is a private file you keep and a public file you share. The server holds your public key and challenges you to prove you own the private one. SSH certificates go further: a certificate authority signs your key for a limited time.","k":[["What limits a stolen SSH certificate?","Its validity window and the principals it lists; after expiry the server rejects it without any cleanup."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c4","t":"sudo and least privilege","p":"sudo lets an approved user run specific commands as another user, usually root, and records each use. The aim is that nobody works as root all day.","k":[["Why edit with visudo?","A syntax error in sudoers can disable sudo for everyone; visudo validates before it saves."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c5","t":"SELinux in practice","p":"SELinux labels every process and file and then checks each access against a policy. Even root can only do what the policy allows for its label. It is the default on RHEL, Fedora and Amazon Linux 2023.","k":[["What is the difference between chmod and an SELinux label?","chmod is discretionary and decided by the owner; the label is enforced by system policy and applies even to root."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c6","t":"AppArmor profiles","p":"AppArmor is the mandatory access control used by Ubuntu, Debian and SUSE. Instead of labelling files it attaches a profile to a program that lists the paths, capabilities and network access it may use.","k":[["Which log would you read to find out why a confined program was blocked?","The kernel log or journal for DENIED entries, then aa-logprof to adjust the profile."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c7","t":"Auditing with auditd","p":"The Linux audit framework records security relevant events in the kernel: file access, command execution, account changes. auditd writes them to /var/log/audit/audit.log.","k":[["Why tag audit rules with -k?","The key lets you search quickly for one rule's events instead of scanning the whole log."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s10-security-hardening-c8","t":"Host firewall and automatic patching","p":"A host firewall decides which network connections reach the machine, and patching closes known holes. Both are routine work that should be automatic.","k":[["Why is replacing a node better than patching it for long lived fleets?","Every node then comes from a known, tested image, and drift between servers disappears."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features","title":"Kernel features behind containers","c":[{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features-c1","t":"A container is a process with restrictions","p":"Take a normal process and change what it can see (namespaces), how much it can use (cgroups), what it may do (capabilities, seccomp, LSM) and what its filesystem looks like (overlayfs). The result is what we call a container.","k":[["Why can a kernel vulnerability break container isolation but not usually VM isolation?","Containers share one kernel; a VM has its own kernel and only the hypervisor is shared."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features-c2","t":"Namespaces","p":"A namespace gives a process its own private view of one kind of system resource. Inside, the process thinks it is alone; outside, the host sees it as an ordinary process.","k":[["Why do containers in one pod reach each other on localhost?","They share the pod's network namespace, so they share interfaces and the port space."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features-c3","t":"cgroups v2 resource limits","p":"Control groups put processes into a tree and apply limits and accounting to each group: memory, CPU, IO and process count. Version 2 has one unified hierarchy under /sys/fs/cgroup.","k":[["What does exit code 137 with reason OOMKilled mean?","The container exceeded memory.max and the kernel sent SIGKILL (128 plus 9)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features-c4","t":"Linux capabilities","p":"Historically root could do everything. Capabilities split that power into about forty separate rights, such as binding low ports or changing the system clock, which can be granted one by one.","k":[["Which capability should make you suspicious in a pod review?","CAP_SYS_ADMIN, because it covers mounts, namespaces and many other privileged operations."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features-c5","t":"seccomp system call filtering","p":"Programs ask the kernel to do things through system calls. A seccomp profile is a list of calls a process may make; any other call is blocked or logged.","k":[["What does seccomp restrict that capabilities do not?","The system calls a process may make at all, regardless of its privileges."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s11-container-kernel-features-c6","t":"overlayfs and image layers","p":"overlayfs stacks several read-only directories (image layers) under one writable directory and presents them as one merged filesystem. This is how container images share data and start instantly.","k":[["Why does deleting a big file in a later Dockerfile layer not shrink the image?","The lower layer still contains it; the delete only adds a whiteout in a new layer."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups","title":"Logging, cron, timers and backups","c":[{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c1","t":"Where Linux logs live","p":"Programs report what they do as log lines. On a modern Linux host they flow into the systemd journal and often also into text files under /var/log.","k":[["Why might journalctl show nothing from before the last reboot?","The journal is stored in memory under /run unless persistent storage is enabled."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c2","t":"Searching the journal","p":"journalctl filters by unit, time, priority and boot, so you can narrow millions of lines to the few that matter.","k":[["How would you see only errors from one service in the last hour?","journalctl -u name -p err --since \"1 hour ago\"."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c3","t":"logrotate and disk safety","p":"Log files grow forever unless something rotates them. logrotate renames old logs, compresses them and deletes the oldest by a policy you define.","k":[["Why signal the app after rotating?","It holds the old file open; the signal makes it reopen a fresh file at the original path."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c4","t":"Central logging and structured logs","p":"Instead of logging into servers to read files, every host sends its logs to one searchable place. Applications write each event as one JSON line with fields rather than free text.","k":[["Why are JSON logs better than plain text at scale?","Fields are indexed and filterable without fragile regular expressions."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c5","t":"cron","p":"cron runs commands on a schedule. Each line has five time fields and then a command.","k":[["Why does a script that works in a terminal fail from cron?","Cron has a minimal environment, so PATH and variables differ; use absolute paths."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c6","t":"systemd timers versus cron","p":"A systemd timer starts a service unit on a schedule. It gives you the logging, resource limits and dependency handling of a service.","k":[["Which setting makes a timer run a job that was missed while the host was off?","Persistent=true."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s12-logging-cron-backups-c7","t":"Backups that can be restored","p":"A backup is a copy of data that lets you recover after deletion, corruption, ransomware or a failed host. A backup you have never restored is only a hope.","k":[["What is the difference between RPO and RTO?","RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time; RTO is the maximum acceptable time to restore service."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive","title":"systemd deep dive","c":[{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c1","t":"Targets and the boot graph","p":"A target is a named goal state made of other units, such as multi-user.target (a normal server) or rescue.target (single-user repair). systemd boots by reaching the default target and starting everything it pulls in.","k":[["What does isolate do that start does not?","It stops every unit not required by the target, switching to exactly that state."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c2","t":"Dependencies and ordering","p":"Two separate ideas: requirement (must this other unit exist and run?) and ordering (who starts first?). They are declared separately.","k":[["A service needs a database on another host at start. Which targets should it use?","`After=network-online.target` and `Wants=network-online.target`, plus retry logic in the app."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c3","t":"Drop-ins and overriding units","p":"Never edit a unit file shipped by a package. A drop-in file in unit.service.d/ overrides only the settings you name, and survives package upgrades.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c4","t":"journald and persistent logs","p":"journald collects output of all services plus kernel messages in a structured, indexed binary journal. journalctl queries it.","k":[["How do you read the logs from before the last reboot?","`journalctl -b -1`, which needs persistent storage."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c5","t":"Resource control with cgroups","p":"Every service runs in its own cgroup, a kernel group that can limit and measure CPU, memory, I/O and process count. systemd exposes these as unit settings.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c6","t":"Socket and path activation","p":"systemd can listen on a port for you and start the service only when the first connection arrives. A path unit starts a service when a file appears or changes.","k":[["Why use socket activation for restarts?","The socket stays open, so clients queue instead of being refused while the process restarts."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s13-systemd-deep-dive-c7","t":"Debugging failed units","p":"When a unit fails, work through status, the journal, the exact environment, and the security settings. Most failures are a wrong path, a wrong user, or a sandbox option that blocks something.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth","title":"SSH in depth","c":[{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c1","t":"How SSH authenticates","p":"SSH first sets up an encrypted channel and checks the server host key, then proves who you are, usually with a key pair: a private key that never leaves your machine and a public key placed on the server.","k":[["What is in authorized_keys on the server?","Public keys allowed to log in as that user; never private keys."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c2","t":"ssh-agent and client configuration","p":"The agent holds your decrypted private key in memory so you type the passphrase once. ~/.ssh/config stores per-host settings so commands stay short.","k":[["Why IdentitiesOnly yes?","It stops the client offering every agent key, which can hit the server's attempt limit."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c3","t":"Bastion hosts and ProxyJump","p":"A bastion (jump host) is the single hardened server reachable from outside. Everything else sits in a private network and is reached through it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c4","t":"Agent forwarding and its risk","p":"Agent forwarding (-A) lets a remote host ask your local agent to sign challenges, so you can hop onward without copying keys.","k":[["Which is safer for reaching a private host, ProxyJump or -A?","ProxyJump."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c5","t":"SSH certificates","p":"An SSH certificate is a public key signed by a certificate authority you control, with an identity, allowed usernames and an expiry. Servers trust the CA instead of individual keys.","k":[["How does a certificate login revoke access?","It expires, so a short validity period limits exposure; a revocation list is also possible."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c6","t":"Tunnels and port forwarding","p":"SSH can carry other TCP traffic inside its encrypted connection. Local forwarding brings a remote port to your laptop, remote forwarding opens a port on the server, and dynamic forwarding makes a SOCKS proxy.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s14-ssh-in-depth-c7","t":"Hardening sshd","p":"Hardening means turning off what you do not use: passwords, root login, old algorithms, and unneeded forwarding, then verifying the effective configuration.","k":[["Why keep an existing session open when reloading sshd?","If the new config blocks logins you can still fix it from the open session."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme","title":"TLS and certificates with openssl and ACME","c":[{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c1","t":"What is inside a certificate","p":"An X.509 certificate is a signed statement: \"this public key belongs to these names, valid between these dates, issued by this authority\".","k":[["Which field does the browser match the hostname against?","The Subject Alternative Name list."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c2","t":"Keys and certificate signing requests","p":"You generate a private key locally and a CSR, a request containing the public key and the names you want. The CA signs it and returns the certificate. The private key never travels.","k":[["Which file must never be sent to the CA?","The private key."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c3","t":"A private CA for test and internal use","p":"For labs and internal services you can act as your own CA: create a root key and certificate, then sign leaf certificates and add the root to the machines that must trust it.","k":[["How does a client trust the leaf from the mini CA?","Add ca.crt to its trust store or point the client at it explicitly."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c4","t":"Verifying chains and serving them","p":"Many outages are \"works in my browser, fails in curl\". The server sent only the leaf and the browser fetched the missing intermediate, but other clients do not.","k":[["What does \"unable to get local issuer certificate\" usually mean?","The client cannot build a path to a trusted root, often a missing intermediate."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c5","t":"ACME and Let's Encrypt","p":"ACME is a protocol that lets software prove control of a domain to a CA and receive a certificate automatically. Let's Encrypt is the best known free CA using it.","k":[["Which challenge supports wildcard names?","DNS-01."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c6","t":"Monitoring expiry and renewal","p":"Automation fails silently: a DNS token expires, a firewall blocks port 80. Alert on days remaining, not on renewal job success alone.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s15-tls-certificates-acme-c7","t":"Common errors and mutual TLS","p":"Most certificate errors are one of five: expired, wrong name, unknown issuer, wrong time on the client, or protocol mismatch. Mutual TLS adds a client certificate so both sides prove identity.","k":[["What does mutual TLS add?","The client also presents a certificate, so the server can authenticate it."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced","title":"Advanced Linux networking","c":[{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c1","t":"Network namespaces","p":"A network namespace is a private copy of the network stack: its own interfaces, routes, firewall rules and ports. Each container or pod gets one.","k":[["Why can two pods both listen on port 8080?","Each pod has its own network namespace and therefore its own port space."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c2","t":"veth pairs","p":"A veth pair is a virtual cable with two ends. Whatever enters one end comes out the other, so you can place each end in a different namespace.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c3","t":"Bridges","p":"A Linux bridge is a virtual switch. Many veth ends attach to it, and they can all talk at layer 2.","k":[["What does MASQUERADE do here?","It rewrites the namespaces' private source addresses to the host address for outbound traffic."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c4","t":"Routing tables and policy routing","p":"The kernel chooses a route by longest prefix match in a routing table. Policy routing adds rules that pick which table to use based on source address, mark or other fields.","k":[["What does \"ip route get\" tell you?","The route, interface and source address the kernel would use for that destination."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c5","t":"Connection tracking","p":"conntrack records every flow the kernel firewall and NAT handle, so reply packets are matched to the right connection and rewritten consistently.","k":[["What symptom indicates a full conntrack table?","New connections time out and the kernel logs \"table full, dropping packet\"."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c6","t":"Traffic control with tc","p":"tc configures queueing disciplines on an interface. With the netem module it can add delay, loss and bandwidth limits, which is a cheap way to test how your application behaves on a bad network.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s16-networking-advanced-c7","t":"Cleaning up and mapping to containers","p":"Everything above is what Docker and Kubernetes automate. Knowing the mapping lets you debug a CNI problem with plain Linux tools.","k":[["How do you run `ss` inside a distroless container's network?","`nsenter -t PID -n ss` from the host uses the host tools in the container's namespace."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability","title":"eBPF and observability tooling","c":[{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c1","t":"What eBPF is","p":"eBPF lets you load small, verified programs into the Linux kernel that run when events happen: a system call, a function entry, a network packet, a timer tick.","k":[["What stops an eBPF program from crashing the kernel?","The verifier rejects unsafe or non-terminating programs before loading."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c2","t":"perf: the first tool to reach for","p":"perf samples what the CPU is doing many times per second and reports which functions use the most time.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c3","t":"Flame graphs","p":"A flame graph turns thousands of stack samples into one picture. Width shows how often a function was on the CPU, and stacking shows who called whom.","k":[["Does the x axis of a flame graph show time?","No, it shows sample counts, with stacks sorted alphabetically."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c4","t":"bpftrace one-liners","p":"bpftrace is a short scripting language for eBPF. A single line can answer a question that used to need a custom tool.","k":[["How do you stop a bpftrace aggregate and see it?","Press Ctrl-C and the map is printed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c5","t":"Latency histograms with BCC tools","p":"The bcc-tools (or libbpf-tools) package ships ready made tracers. Each is a tested program for one question, with names like biolatency and tcpconnect.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c6","t":"Observability in Kubernetes with eBPF","p":"On a Kubernetes node eBPF tools see every pod because they share the kernel. Projects wrap them so you get per pod answers without changing images.","k":[["Why can one eBPF agent per node observe all pods?","Pods share the node's kernel, where the programs attach."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s17-ebpf-observability-c7","t":"Choosing the right tool and staying safe","p":"Start with cheap tools and escalate only if needed. Tracing has overhead, and a careless probe on a hot function can slow production.","k":[["Which is safer on a busy host, sampling or tracing every event?","Sampling, because overhead is bounded by the rate."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning","title":"Kernel tuning","c":[{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c1","t":"sysctl and persistent settings","p":"sysctl reads and writes kernel parameters, which appear as files under /proc/sys. Changes made at runtime are lost on reboot unless saved in a config file.","k":[["How does a sysctl survive reboot?","It is placed in a file under /etc/sysctl.d and loaded at boot."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c2","t":"Memory tuning: swappiness, dirty pages and overcommit","p":"vm.swappiness sets how eagerly the kernel swaps process memory out in favour of page cache. Dirty-page settings control when written data is flushed to disk. Overcommit controls whether the kernel allows promising more memory than exists.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c3","t":"Hugepages and transparent hugepages","p":"Memory is mapped in pages of 4 KiB. A hugepage is 2 MiB (or 1 GiB), so fewer mappings and fewer TLB misses are needed for large memory users.","k":[["Why is THP sometimes disabled for databases?","Background compaction can cause latency spikes; vendors often recommend turning it off."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c4","t":"NUMA","p":"On multi-socket servers each CPU has memory attached to it. Access to local memory is faster than access to memory attached to another socket (NUMA: non-uniform memory access).","k":[["What does a high numa_miss count suggest?","Processes are using memory from a remote node, adding latency."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c5","t":"I/O schedulers and block device settings","p":"The I/O scheduler orders requests to a disk. Modern blk-mq kernels offer none, mq-deadline, bfq and kyber.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c6","t":"File descriptors and process limits","p":"Every open file, socket and pipe uses a file descriptor. Limits exist per process and per system. Hitting them gives \"too many open files\".","k":[["Where do you raise the limit for a systemd service?","`LimitNOFILE=` in the unit or a drop-in."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s18-kernel-tuning-c7","t":"Network stack tuning","p":"A few parameters matter for busy servers: listen queue length, ephemeral port range, buffer sizes and congestion control.","k":[["How much data can be in flight on a 1 Gbit/s, 50 ms path?","1e9 x 0.05 / 8 = 6,250,000 bytes."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery","title":"Boot process, GRUB and recovery","c":[{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c1","t":"The boot sequence","p":"Booting is a relay: firmware starts a boot loader, which loads the kernel and an initial RAM disk, which mounts the real root filesystem, which starts systemd.","k":[["Which stage loads the drivers needed to mount root?","The initramfs."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c2","t":"UEFI, the ESP and partitions","p":"UEFI systems keep boot loaders on a small FAT-formatted EFI System Partition (ESP), usually mounted at /boot/efi. Legacy BIOS systems use the first sector of the disk.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c3","t":"GRUB configuration and kernel parameters","p":"GRUB reads /boot/grub/grub.cfg, which is generated. You edit /etc/default/grub and regenerate; you do not edit grub.cfg by hand.","k":[["Why not edit grub.cfg directly?","It is generated and will be overwritten; edit /etc/default/grub and regenerate."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c4","t":"Rescue and emergency modes","p":"systemd has two repair targets. rescue.target mounts local filesystems and starts basic services with a root shell. emergency.target mounts only the root filesystem read-only and starts almost nothing.","k":[["How do you verify fstab changes without rebooting?","Run `mount -a`, which tries every entry."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c5","t":"initramfs","p":"The initramfs is a small compressed filesystem loaded with the kernel. It contains just enough modules and scripts to find and unlock the root device.","k":[["What error suggests a missing driver in initramfs?","Kernel panic, unable to mount root fs, or a drop to the initramfs shell."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c6","t":"Repairing GRUB from a live system or chroot","p":"If the boot loader is damaged, boot another system, mount the broken one, chroot into it and reinstall GRUB.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s19-boot-grub-recovery-c7","t":"Cloud instances that will not boot","p":"In the cloud you have no physical console but you do have the serial console and the ability to move a disk to another instance.","k":[["Why snapshot the volume before rescuing?","So a mistake during the repair can be undone."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos","title":"Building packages and running repositories","c":[{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c1","t":"What a package is","p":"A package is an archive of files plus metadata: name, version, dependencies and scripts to run on install and removal. The package manager tracks what it installed so it can upgrade and remove cleanly.","k":[["Which tool resolves dependencies, dpkg or apt?","apt; dpkg installs a given file without fetching dependencies."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c2","t":"Versioning and dependencies","p":"Package versions follow a scheme the manager can compare. Dependencies state which other packages and versions must be present.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c3","t":"Building a deb package with a control file","p":"A minimal deb needs a directory tree mirroring the install paths and a DEBIAN/control file. dpkg-deb --build turns it into a package.","k":[["Where does pkg/usr/local/bin/hello install?","/usr/local/bin/hello."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c4","t":"Building an rpm package with a spec file","p":"An rpm is described by a spec file: metadata, build steps, a file list and a changelog. rpmbuild follows the steps in a clean build root.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c5","t":"fpm and packaging in CI","p":"fpm converts a directory, gem, Python package or tarball into deb or rpm with one command. It is popular for wrapping existing binaries, for example a Go program.","k":[["Why test the package in a clean container?","The build machine hides missing dependencies that a fresh host would reveal."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c6","t":"Creating an APT repository","p":"A repository is a directory of packages plus index files listing them, served over HTTP(S). Clients download the index, then the packages.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s20-package-building-repos-c7","t":"Signing and trust","p":"A package signature proves it came from the holder of a private key and was not altered. Clients check it against a public key they trust.","k":[["What does gpgcheck=1 do?","The client refuses packages that do not have a valid signature from a trusted key."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible","title":"Configuration management and Ansible basics","c":[{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c1","t":"Desired state and idempotence","p":"You describe what the server should look like (package installed, file present, service running). The tool changes only what differs. Running it twice makes no further changes: that property is idempotence.","k":[["What does an idempotent run report on the second execution?","Zero changed tasks."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c2","t":"Inventory and ad hoc commands","p":"The inventory lists the hosts and groups Ansible manages. Ad hoc commands run one module on many hosts without writing a playbook.","k":[["Which flag runs a task with sudo?","`-b` (become)."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c3","t":"Playbooks, tasks and modules","p":"A playbook is a YAML file listing plays; each play maps hosts to ordered tasks; each task calls a module such as package, template or service.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c4","t":"Variables, templates and facts","p":"Variables make a playbook reusable. Templates (Jinja2) generate config files from variables. Facts are values Ansible discovers on each host, like OS family or memory.","k":[["Why prefer the template module to echo in shell?","It is idempotent and shows the diff."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c5","t":"Handlers and ordering","p":"A handler is a task that runs only when notified by a task that changed something, and only once at the end of the play.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c6","t":"Roles and collections","p":"A role is a standard folder layout that packages tasks, defaults, templates and handlers for one purpose, such as nginx or nodeexporter. Collections distribute roles and modules.","k":[["Where do default values for a role live?","defaults/main.yml."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s21-configuration-management-ansible-c7","t":"Secrets, testing and Ansible with Kubernetes","p":"Ansible Vault encrypts variable files so secrets can live in Git. Testing with --check, ansible-lint and Molecule catches errors before production.","k":[["What does serial: 2 do?","It runs the play on two hosts at a time, limiting blast radius."]],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity","title":"Shell productivity","c":[{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c1","t":"tmux sessions, windows and panes","p":"tmux keeps terminal sessions running on the server after you disconnect. Inside one session you can have several windows and split panes.","k":[["What happens to a tmux session when SSH disconnects?","It keeps running on the server and you can reattach."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c2","t":"vim survival and speed","p":"vi or vim exists on every server. It has modes: normal mode for commands, insert mode for typing, command-line mode for save and quit.","k":[["How do you quit without saving?","Press Esc, then :q! and Enter."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c3","t":"find recipes","p":"find walks directories and tests each file by name, type, size, age, owner or permission, then optionally acts on it.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c4","t":"xargs and safe pipelines","p":"xargs takes lines from input and turns them into arguments for another command, in batches or in parallel.","k":[["Why use -print0 with -0?","Names with spaces or newlines stay intact instead of being split."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c5","t":"Regular expressions","p":"A regular expression (regex) describes a text pattern. It lets you search, validate and replace without listing every possibility.","k":[],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c6","t":"History, shortcuts and shell habits","p":"The shell remembers commands and offers editing shortcuts that save thousands of keystrokes.","k":[["What does sudo !! do?","Re-runs the previous command with sudo."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s22-shell-productivity-c7","t":"Text pipelines for operations","p":"Combining grep, sort, uniq, cut, awk and jq answers most log questions in one line.","k":[],"o":[]}]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs","title":"Incident labs","c":[{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c1","t":"The triage routine","p":"Before diving into one theory, spend two minutes collecting the same facts every time. It prevents tunnel vision and produces notes for the postmortem.","k":[["Why capture state before restarting?","A restart destroys evidence such as memory use, open files and error state."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c2","t":"Lab 1: the disk is full","p":"Writes fail with \"No space left on device\". Services crash, databases refuse writes and logs stop. There are two kinds: out of blocks, and out of inodes.","k":[["df shows 100 percent but du totals far less. Why?","A process still holds deleted files open; find with lsof +L1."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c3","t":"Lab 2: a memory leak","p":"Memory use of one process climbs steadily until the OOM killer ends it or the host starts to swap. The classic pattern is a sawtooth on the dashboard: rises, crash, restart, rises again.","k":[["What exit code does a container OOM kill report?","137, which is 128 plus signal 9."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c4","t":"Lab 3: a DNS outage","p":"Names stop resolving, so everything that uses a hostname fails even though the network is up. In Kubernetes, CoreDNS problems look like random timeouts across all services.","k":[["ping by IP works but by name fails. What layer is broken?","Name resolution (DNS), not routing."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c5","t":"Lab 4: a runaway process","p":"One process uses all the CPU, or spawns children until the process table fills (a fork bomb), and the machine becomes unresponsive.","k":[["Why STOP before KILL?","It freezes the process reversibly so you can inspect it without losing evidence."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c6","t":"Lab 5: an expired certificate","p":"At a known second, HTTPS connections start failing with \"certificate has expired\". Browsers show a full-page warning and APIs return TLS errors.","k":[["After renewing the file, nginx still serves the old certificate. Why?","It loaded the certificate at start; it needs a reload."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c7","t":"Lab 6: inode exhaustion and file descriptor limits","p":"Two quiet limits that look like a full disk or a broken network: no free inodes, and no free file descriptors.","k":[["Which state count rising points to a socket leak in the application?","CLOSE_WAIT, meaning the app has not closed sockets the peer closed."]],"o":[]},{"id":"s23-incident-labs-c8","t":"After the incident: runbook and prevention","p":"Every lab ends the same way: write down what happened, add a detection, and add the guard rail so it cannot recur silently.","k":[["Which is better, an alert on 90 percent disk or on predicted exhaustion in 4 hours?","The prediction, because it measures the time you have left rather than a fixed level."]],"o":[]}]}]};